#{[17592186088897 "Given a matrix $A = \n\t\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe have been using the following formula to compute the inverse:\n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{ad-bc}\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo check that this formula is correct for a general matrix $A$, we need to verify that\n\n$$\nAA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I_2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $A^{-1}A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}\\cdot A & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc & 0 \\\\ 0 & -bc+ad\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $A A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc & 0 \\\\ 0 & -bc+ad\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have verified that\n\n$$\nAA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I_2,\n$$\n\nand we can be sure that the given formula is the correct formula for $A^{-1}.$"] [17592186116490 "Matrices can be used to represent systems of equations, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2}x+{\\color{red}3}y &={\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{red}4}x\\text{ }{\\color{red}-}\\text{ }{\\color{red}5}y &={\\color{blue}-1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\times 2$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $2\\times 2$ matrix, called the **coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}4} & {\\color{red}-5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $2\\times 1$ column vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1}\\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}4} & {\\color{red}-5}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can write any system of two equations with two variables in this form.\nJust to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}4} & {\\color{red}-5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2}x+{\\color{red}3}y \\\\ {\\color{red}4}x\\text{ }{\\color{red}-}\\text{ }{\\color{red}5}y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}5} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =b\\,{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186121025 "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 5 & 12 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}-2} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ -5 & {\\color{gray}∗} & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\nM_{32}=\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods."]}
